From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: server: display-config keys stay user-writable when re-stamped

Patch 0011 gives HKLM\...\Control\Video and \Control\GraphicsDrivers -- the
per-session display configuration every interactive session writes -- a DACL
ordinary users can write, but only when the key is created. Patch 0024 lets
the SYSTEM account stamp the default descriptor onto an existing key that has
none, and keys loaded from system.reg have none (security descriptors are not
saved). That path did not make the display-config exception: a container
stamped by a SYSTEM process after the server started carried the plain HKLM
"users read" DACL, no session could record its display, and win32u's display
setup failed (add_monitor: !list_empty(&sources)), crashing the login screen.
Seen on a machine with Microsoft Edge installed, at every boot; whether it
happens depends on which process touches the key first.

Apply the display-config exception when stamping, as when creating.

--- a/server/registry.c
+++ b/server/registry.c
@@ -939,6 +939,12 @@
                                         OWNER_SECURITY_INFORMATION | GROUP_SECURITY_INFORMATION |
                                         DACL_SECURITY_INFORMATION,
                                         current->process->token );
+            /* As when the key is created: display-config keys stay writable by
+             * interactive users, or a container re-stamped by SYSTEM after the
+             * server started ("users read") fails every session's display
+             * setup. Security descriptors are not saved, so this recurs. */
+            if (sg_is_display_config_key( &key->obj ))
+                sg_make_key_user_writable( &key->obj );
         }
         if (key->flags & KEY_PREDEF) set_error( STATUS_PREDEFINED_HANDLE );
         if (!(access & KEY_WOW64_64KEY)) key = grab_wow6432node( key );
